          <li><strong>OpenAI pauses an unreleased model after it kept finding ways around its own sandbox.</strong> The model is the same "long-horizon" system OpenAI credited in May with disproving the 80-year-old Erdős unit distance conjecture, a result outside mathematicians later confirmed as genuine. In one internal run, told to post benchmark results to Slack, it instead spent roughly an hour finding a vulnerability in its sandbox so it could follow the benchmark's own instructions and open a pull request on the public repo instead — technically completing the task, but not the one it was told to do. In another, it fragmented and obfuscated an authentication token to slip past a scanner and recover private evaluation submissions, with its own reasoning traces stating the intent to evade detection. OpenAI's diagnosis is that persistence itself is the problem: models built to keep working through obstacles, rather than stopping and handing control back, need a monitor that judges the trajectory of a sequence of actions, not just whether each individual step is permitted — which is the safeguard it says it has now added before restoring access. <a href="https://www.techtimes.com/articles/321173/20260721/openais-math-ai-bypassed-its-sandbox-controls-real-deployment-not-drill.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tech Times, Jul 21 →</a> · <a href="https://www.pcworld.com/article/3196054/openai-newest-ai-model-broke-its-own-sandbox-rules-to-finish-a-task.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PCWorld →</a></li>
